{
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "maxima",
      "display_name": "Maxima",
      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# The 2D Ising Model\n\nThe Ising model places a spin $s = \\pm 1$ on every site of a square lattice and couples each spin to its four neighbours. In two dimensions it orders spontaneously below a critical temperature, and Onsager solved it exactly in 1944. This notebook derives the critical temperature and the exact magnetisation, energy and heat capacity symbolically, then simulates the model with the Metropolis algorithm and compares the two.\n\nThe simulation runs in compiled Maxima code on lattices of $12 \\times 12$ and $24 \\times 24$ spins. Finite lattices cannot have a true phase transition, so the measured curves round off near $T_c$. The last sections measure that rounding and use the Binder cumulant to locate $T_c$ from the finite lattices.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The Hamiltonian and the Metropolis Rule [S]\n\nWith coupling $J = 1$, Boltzmann's constant $k_B = 1$ and no external field, the energy of a configuration is\n\n$$H = -\\sum_{\\langle ij \\rangle} s_i s_j$$\n\nwhere the sum runs over nearest-neighbour pairs, each counted once. Flipping spin $s_i$ changes the energy by $\\Delta E = 2 s_i h_i$, where $h_i$ is the sum of its four neighbours. The Metropolis rule accepts the flip with probability $\\min(1, e^{-\\Delta E / T})$, which satisfies detailed balance with respect to the Boltzmann distribution. For $s_i = +1$ the neighbour sum takes only five values, so only two acceptance probabilities ever need an exponential.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "rows : makelist([h, 2*h, if 2*h <= 0 then 1 else exp(-2*h/T)], h, [-4, -2, 0, 2, 4])$\napply(matrix, cons([\"h\", \"ΔE\", \"acceptance\"], rows));",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined\\pmatrix{\\else\\begin{pmatrix}\\fi \\mbox{ h }& \\mbox{ ΔE }&\\mbox{ acceptance }\\cr -4&-8&1\\cr -2&-4&1\\cr 0&0&1\\cr 2&4&e^ {- \\left({{4}\\over{T}}\\right) }\\cr 4&8&e^ {- \\left({{8}\\over{T}}\\right) }\\cr \\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined}\\else\\end{pmatrix}\\fi "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 2,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The simulation precomputes $e^{-4/T}$ and $e^{-8/T}$ once per temperature and compares a uniform random number against them.\n\n## Onsager's Critical Temperature [S]\n\nKramers and Wannier showed that the high-temperature and low-temperature expansions of the partition function map onto each other under $\\sinh(2K)\\sinh(2K^*) = 1$, with $K = J/T$. The transition sits at the self-dual point $\\sinh(2K_c) = 1$, which Onsager's solution confirms.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "K_c : logarc(rhs(first(solve(sinh(2*K) = 1, K))))$\nT_c : 1/K_c$\nTc_f : float(T_c)$\nprintf(true, \"K_c = ~,10f   T_c = ~,10f~%\", float(K_c), Tc_f)$\n['K_c = K_c, 'T_c = T_c];",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "K_c = 0.4406867935   T_c = 2.2691853142"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ {\\it K\\_c}={{\\log \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1\\right)}\\over{2}} , {\\it T\\_c}={{2 }\\over{\\log \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1\\right)}} \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 3,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Spontaneous Magnetisation [S]\n\nOnsager (announced 1948) and Yang (proved 1952) found the magnetisation per spin below $T_c$:\n\n$$m(T) = \\left(1 - \\sinh^{-4}(2/T)\\right)^{1/8}$$\n\nIt vanishes at $T_c$ because $\\sinh(2/T_c) = 1$, and the exponent $1/8$ is the critical exponent $\\beta$. Writing $x = e^{-2/T}$ turns it into a series at low temperature. The leading correction, $-2x^4 = -2e^{-8/T}$, is the cost of isolated flipped spins: each costs energy 8 and lowers the total magnetisation by 2.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "m_ons : (1 - sinh(2/T)^(-4))^(1/8)$\nprint(\"sinh(2/T_c) =\", radcan(exponentialize(subst(T = T_c, sinh(2/T)))))$\nm_x : subst(T = -2/log(x), exponentialize(m_ons))$\ntaylor(m_x, x, 0, 10);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "sinh(2/T_c) = 1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "1-2\\,x^4-8\\,x^6-34\\,x^8-152\\,x^{10}+\\cdots "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 4,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Exact Energy and Heat Capacity [S]\n\nOnsager's energy per spin involves the complete elliptic integral of the first kind $K(m)$ (Maxima's `elliptic_kc`, which takes the parameter $m = \\kappa^2$):\n\n$$u(T) = -\\coth(2/T)\\left[1 + \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\left(2\\tanh^2(2/T) - 1\\right) K(\\kappa^2)\\right], \\qquad \\kappa = \\frac{2\\sinh(2/T)}{\\cosh^2(2/T)}$$\n\nThe heat capacity per spin is $c = du/dT$, which Maxima differentiates through the elliptic integral. At $T_c$ the modulus reaches $\\kappa = 1$, where $K$ diverges logarithmically. The prefactor $2\\tanh^2 - 1$ vanishes there, so $u(T_c) = -\\coth(2K_c)$ stays finite while $c$ diverges.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "kappa : 2*sinh(2/T)/cosh(2/T)^2$\nu_ons : -coth(2/T)*(1 + (2/%pi)*(2*tanh(2/T)^2 - 1)*elliptic_kc(kappa^2))$\nc_ons : diff(u_ons, T)$\nat_Tc : ratsimp(radcan(exponentialize(subst(T = T_c, [kappa, 2*tanh(2/T)^2 - 1, -coth(2/T)]))),\n                algebraic : true)$\nprintf(true, \"At T_c: kappa = ~a, 2 tanh^2 - 1 = ~a, u = ~a = ~,6f~%\",\n       at_Tc[1], at_Tc[2], at_Tc[3], float(at_Tc[3]))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "At T_c: kappa = 1, 2 tanh^2 - 1 = 0, u = -sqrt(2) = -1.414214"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A Compiled Metropolis Sweep [N]\n\nAn interpreted Maxima loop takes tens of microseconds per operation, too slow for millions of spin updates. The sweep below declares every variable's type with `mode_declare` and passes the lattice as a `fixnum` array, so `compile` turns it into Lisp with typed integer arithmetic. Three choices made it about ten times faster than a direct translation:\n\n- The lattice is a one-dimensional array with site $(i, j)$ at index $iL + j$. Maxima's array access collects a second index into a list on every call, and a single index avoids that.\n- Periodic neighbours come from lookup tables `up` and `dn`, because the translator leaves `mod` as a generic call.\n- The loops end with an explicit `return`, because the translator also leaves the end test of `thru` generic.\n\nThe sweep visits the sites in order, row by row. Each single-spin Metropolis step leaves the Boltzmann distribution unchanged, so a sequence of them does too. The timing run measures the cost before choosing the scope of the experiment.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "set_random_state(make_random_state(42))$\n\nmetropolis_sweep(a, up, dn, n, p4, p8) := block([i:0, j:0, r:0, ru:0, rd:0, k:0, s0:0, h:0],\n  mode_declare([n, i, j, r, ru, rd, k, s0, h], fixnum, [p4, p8], float,\n               completearray(a), fixnum, completearray(up), fixnum, completearray(dn), fixnum),\n  for i : 0 step 1 do (\n    if i >= n then return(0),\n    r : i*n, ru : up[i]*n, rd : dn[i]*n,\n    for j : 0 step 1 do (\n      if j >= n then return(0),\n      k : r + j,\n      s0 : a[k],\n      /* h = s0 * (neighbour sum), so the energy change is 2*h */\n      h : s0*(a[ru + j] + a[rd + j] + a[r + up[j]] + a[r + dn[j]]),\n      if h <= 0 then a[k] : -s0\n      else if h = 2 then (if random(1.0) < p4 then a[k] : -s0)\n      else (if random(1.0) < p8 then a[k] : -s0))),\n  0)$\ncompile(metropolis_sweep)$\n\n/* An n x n lattice with all spins up, plus the periodic neighbour tables */\nnew_lattice(n) := block([a : make_array(fixnum, n*n), up : make_array(fixnum, n),\n                         dn : make_array(fixnum, n)],\n  fillarray(a, [1]),\n  for i : 0 thru n - 1 do (up[i] : mod(i + 1, n), dn[i] : mod(i - 1, n)),\n  [a, up, dn])$\n\nlat : new_lattice(24)$\nt0 : elapsed_real_time()$\nfor r : 1 thru 2000 do metropolis_sweep(lat[1], lat[2], lat[3], 24, exp(-4/2.3), exp(-8/2.3))$\ndt : elapsed_real_time() - t0$\nprintf(true, \"~,3f microseconds per spin update, ~,3f ms per sweep of a 24 x 24 lattice~%\",\n       1.0e6*dt/(2000*24^2), 1.0e3*dt/2000)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "0.190 microseconds per spin update, 0.110 ms per sweep of a 24 x 24 lattice"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Measuring Energy and Magnetisation [N]\n\nAfter each sweep the run computes the total energy $E$ and the magnetisation per spin $|M|/N$ in one pass over the lattice, and accumulates their first, second and fourth moments. The heat capacity per spin follows from the energy fluctuations,\n\n$$c = \\frac{\\langle E^2 \\rangle - \\langle E \\rangle^2}{N T^2}$$\n\nand the Binder cumulant is $U = 1 - \\langle m^4 \\rangle / (3\\langle m^2 \\rangle^2)$. The run discards `neq` sweeps for equilibration before measuring over `nms` sweeps. It returns $[T, \\langle|m|\\rangle, \\langle E\\rangle/N, c, U]$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ising_run(a, up, dn, n, T, neq, nms) := block([p4:0.0, p8:0.0, t:0, i:0, j:0, r:0,\n    ie:0, im:0, e:0.0, m:0.0, N:0.0, se:0.0, se2:0.0, sm:0.0, sm2:0.0, sm4:0.0],\n  mode_declare([n, neq, nms, t, i, j, r, ie, im], fixnum,\n               [T, p4, p8, e, m, N, se, se2, sm, sm2, sm4], float,\n               completearray(a), fixnum, completearray(up), fixnum, completearray(dn), fixnum),\n  p4 : exp(-4.0/T), p8 : exp(-8.0/T), N : float(n*n),\n  for t : 1 step 1 do (if t > neq then return(0), metropolis_sweep(a, up, dn, n, p4, p8)),\n  for t : 1 step 1 do (\n    if t > nms then return(0),\n    metropolis_sweep(a, up, dn, n, p4, p8),\n    /* each bond counted once: right and down neighbours */\n    ie : 0, im : 0,\n    for i : 0 step 1 do (\n      if i >= n then return(0),\n      r : i*n,\n      for j : 0 step 1 do (\n        if j >= n then return(0),\n        ie : ie - a[r + j]*(a[up[i]*n + j] + a[r + up[j]]),\n        im : im + a[r + j])),\n    e : float(ie), m : abs(float(im))/N,\n    se : se + e, se2 : se2 + e*e,\n    sm : sm + m, sm2 : sm2 + m*m, sm4 : sm4 + m*m*m*m),\n  se : se/nms, se2 : se2/nms, sm : sm/nms, sm2 : sm2/nms, sm4 : sm4/nms,\n  [T, sm, se/N, (se2 - se*se)/(T*T*N), 1.0 - sm4/(3.0*sm2*sm2)])$\ncompile(ising_run)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Spin Configurations [N]\n\nSnapshots of a larger $64 \\times 64$ lattice show what the averages summarise. Below $T_c$ the lattice starts fully ordered. Near and above $T_c$ it starts from random spins. Each snapshot follows 1500 sweeps. At $T = 1.8$ almost every spin points up, with small islands of flipped spins; the exact magnetisation there is $m = 0.959$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "snapshot(n, T, start, nsweeps) := block([lat : new_lattice(n), a],\n  a : lat[1],\n  if start = 'random then for k : 0 thru n*n - 1 do a[k] : 2*random(2) - 1,\n  for r : 1 thru nsweeps do\n    metropolis_sweep(a, lat[2], lat[3], n, float(exp(-4/T)), float(exp(-8/T))),\n  genmatrix(lambda([i, j], a[(i - 1)*n + j - 1]), n, n))$\n\nsnaps : [snapshot(64, 1.8, 'ordered, 1500),\n         snapshot(64, 2.3, 'random, 1500),\n         snapshot(64, 3.0, 'random, 1500)]$\nfor k : 1 thru 3 do\n  printf(true, \"T = ~,3f: |m| = ~,3f~%\", [1.8, 2.3, 3.0][k],\n         abs(lsum(v, v, flatten(args(snaps[k]))))/64.0^2)$\n\nax_draw2d(ax_heatmap(snaps[1]), colorscale=\"RdBu\", showscale=false, aspect_ratio=true,\n          title=\"T = 1.8, below T_c\", xlabel=\"column\", ylabel=\"row\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "T = 1.800: |m| = 0.950\nT = 2.300: |m| = 0.585\nT = 3.000: |m| = 0.037"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
