    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Temperature Scan [N]\n\nThe timing above sets the scope. Two sizes, $L = 12$ and $L = 24$, run at 15 temperatures, spaced 0.05 apart between 2.2 and 2.5 where the curves change fastest. Each temperature gets 1000 equilibration sweeps and 12000 measured sweeps, and starts from the previous temperature's final configuration. The $L = 24$ lattice costs four times as much per sweep, so its scan runs in two cells, below and above $T = 2.3$. The scans take about 35 seconds in all. A third size of $L = 48$ would have added two minutes, so the notebook leaves it out.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "Ts : [1.5, 1.8, 2.0, 2.1, 2.2, 2.25, 2.3, 2.35, 2.4, 2.45, 2.5, 2.6, 2.8, 3.1, 3.5]$\nnequil : 1000$\nnmeas : 12000$\n/* Run lattice lat at each temperature in temps, carrying the configuration along */\ntemperature_scan(lat, n, temps) :=\n  makelist(ising_run(lat[1], lat[2], lat[3], n, T, nequil, nmeas), T, temps)$\ncolumn(res, k) := map(lambda([r], r[k]), res)$\n\nt0 : elapsed_real_time()$\nres12 : temperature_scan(new_lattice(12), 12, Ts)$\nprintf(true, \"L = 12, 15 temperatures: ~,1f s~%\", elapsed_real_time() - t0)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "L = 12, 15 temperatures: 7.2 s"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "t0 : elapsed_real_time()$\nlat24 : new_lattice(24)$\nres24_low : temperature_scan(lat24, 24, sublist(Ts, lambda([t], t < 2.3)))$\nprintf(true, \"L = 24, T < 2.3: ~,1f s~%\", elapsed_real_time() - t0)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "L = 24, T < 2.3: 11.6 s"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "t0 : elapsed_real_time()$\nres24 : append(res24_low, temperature_scan(lat24, 24, sublist(Ts, lambda([t], t >= 2.3))))$\nprintf(true, \"L = 24, T >= 2.3: ~,1f s~%~%\", elapsed_real_time() - t0)$\n\nprintf(true, \"    T   |m| L=12  |m| L=24   e L=24   c L=12   c L=24~%\")$\nfor k : 1 thru length(Ts) do\n  printf(true, \"~5,2f   ~7,4f   ~7,4f   ~7,4f   ~6,3f   ~6,3f~%\", Ts[k],\n         res12[k][2], res24[k][2], res24[k][3], res12[k][4], res24[k][4])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "L = 24, T >= 2.3: 16.5 s\n    T   |m| L=12  |m| L=24   e L=24   c L=12   c L=24\n 1.50    0.9866    0.9866   -1.9514    0.197    0.196\n 1.80    0.9578    0.9570   -1.8593    0.422    0.443\n 2.00    0.9111    0.9129   -1.7477    0.725    0.695\n 2.10    0.8713    0.8671   -1.6609    0.933    1.002\n 2.20    0.8023    0.7788   -1.5430    1.210    1.454\n 2.25    0.7571    0.7222   -1.4733    1.316    1.515\n 2.30    0.7087    0.6184   -1.3851    1.387    1.734\n 2.35    0.6544    0.5178   -1.3095    1.388    1.647\n 2.40    0.5936    0.3850   -1.2165    1.336    1.319\n 2.45    0.5308    0.3133   -1.1606    1.237    1.110\n 2.50    0.4791    0.2604   -1.1086    1.130    0.940\n 2.60    0.3965    0.2014   -1.0308    0.870    0.731\n 2.80    0.2888    0.1437   -0.9087    0.573    0.512\n 3.10    0.2178    0.1053   -0.7800    0.370    0.348\n 3.50    0.1631    0.0824   -0.6626    0.243    0.252"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Magnetisation Against Onsager [S+N]\n\nThe measured $\\langle|m|\\rangle$ follows Onsager's curve below $T_c$ and the two sizes agree there. Near $T_c$ the curves round off instead of dropping to zero with infinite slope, and above $T_c$ they decay slowly instead of vanishing. The tail is a finite-size effect: a lattice of $N$ uncorrelated spins has $|M| \\sim \\sqrt{N}$, so $\\langle|m|\\rangle$ falls off as $1/L$, and the $L = 24$ tail lies below the $L = 12$ one.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "m_f(t) := float(subst(T = t, m_ons))$\nT_ons : append(makelist(1.2 + (0.9999*Tc_f - 1.2)*i/200, i, 0, 200), [Tc_f, 3.6])$\nm_exact : append(makelist(m_f(t), t, rest(T_ons, -2)), [0.0, 0.0])$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"black\", name=\"Onsager\", lines(T_ons, m_exact),\n  color=\"darkorange\", marker_size=7, name=\"L = 12\", points(Ts, column(res12, 2)),\n  color=\"steelblue\", name=\"L = 24\", points(Ts, column(res24, 2)),\n  title=\"Magnetisation per Spin\", xlabel=\"T\", ylabel=\"<|m|>\",\n  xrange=[1.2, 3.6], showlegend=true)$\n\nprintf(true, \"    T   MC L=24    Onsager    difference~%\")$\nfor k : 1 thru 3 do\n  printf(true, \"~5,2f   ~7,4f    ~7,4f    ~8,4f~%\", Ts[k], res24[k][2], m_f(Ts[k]),\n         res24[k][2] - m_f(Ts[k]))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "    T   MC L=24    Onsager    difference\n 1.50    0.9866     0.9865      0.0001\n 1.80    0.9570     0.9569      0.0001\n 2.00    0.9129     0.9113      0.0016"
          ]
        },
