4649413934297,0.3035779765680835,0.3007356308253455,0.2979369080863819,0.2951808421974917,0.292466495778968,0.28979295912841907,0.2871593491754982,0.2845648084851585,0.2820085043067686,0.27948962766655877,0.27700739250107664,0.2745610348294121,0.2721498119621608,0.26977300174516994,0.2674299018362443,0.26511982901311676,0.2628421185110658,0.2605961233886738,0.25838121392030355,0.2561967770139536,0.2540422156532336,0.25191694836227485,0.24982040869244704,0.24775204472983092,0.24571131862245205,0.2436977061263251,0.24171069616942784,0.23974979043275882,0.23781450294768441,0.23590435970882268,0.23401889830175537,0.23215766754488873,0.23032022714482664,0.22850614736465422],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"black\",\"width\":2},\"name\":\"Onsager\"},{\"x\":[1.5,1.8,2.0,2.1,2.2,2.25,2.3,2.35,2.4,2.45,2.5,2.6,2.8,3.1,3.5],\"y\":[0.19655455555551238,0.4222395002190953,0.7250167777777834,0.9334117011001961,1.2104743838320045,1.3155502825788679,1.3867771704123881,1.388110155285049,1.335515303363981,1.236868727407814,1.1298927850617313,0.8699707537758387,0.5731007407997954,0.3701310825111449,0.24279867214663722],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"darkorange\",\"size\":7},\"name\":\"L = 12\"},{\"x\":[1.5,1.8,2.0,2.1,2.2,2.25,2.3,2.35,2.4,2.45,2.5,2.6,2.8,3.1,3.5],\"y\":[0.1960976988170224,0.4434941938514469,0.6954747037037426,1.0022696659090937,1.4542655723905848,1.5146093455265677,1.7338821557970803,1.647495147481673,1.3188974011582055,1.1103342832654375,0.9401708518518556,0.7312292889135596,0.5123755190371709,0.3475107731625955,0.25158106569665145],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"steelblue\",\"size\":7},\"name\":\"L = 24\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Heat Capacity per Spin\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"width\":700,\"height\":450,\"plot_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"T\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"c\"},\"range\":[0.0,3.5],\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The peak of each measured curve lies between grid points. A parabola through the highest point and its two neighbours locates it. Its vertex follows symbolically from the three points.\n\nFinite-size scaling explains the rounding. Near $T_c$ the correlation length grows as $\\xi \\sim |T - T_c|^{-\\nu}$ with $\\nu = 1$ in two dimensions. Once $\\xi$ reaches $L$ the lattice cannot tell it is near a transition, so the peak shifts by an amount of order $T_c/L$. Because $\\nu = 1$, the peak height grows as $\\ln L$ with the same amplitude as the infinite-lattice divergence $c \\approx -A \\ln|1 - T/T_c|$, where $A = (2/\\pi)(2K_c)^2$. Doubling $L$ should raise the peak by $A \\ln 2$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "par : a0 + a1*x + a2*x^2$\nabc : linsolve([subst(x = x1, par) = y1, subst(x = x2, par) = y2, subst(x = x3, par) = y3],\n               [a0, a1, a2])$\nx_v : ratsimp(subst(abc, -a1/(2*a2)))$\ny_v : ratsimp(subst(abc, a0 - a1^2/(4*a2)))$\n\npeak(res) := block([cs : column(res, 4), k],\n  k : first(sublist_indices(cs, lambda([v], v = lmax(cs)))),\n  subst([x1 = Ts[k-1], x2 = Ts[k], x3 = Ts[k+1], y1 = cs[k-1], y2 = cs[k], y3 = cs[k+1]],\n        [x_v, y_v]))$\n\npk : [peak(res12), peak(res24)]$\nprintf(true, \"  L   T_peak   T_peak - T_c   L (T_peak - T_c)/T_c   c_peak~%\")$\nfor k : 1 thru 2 do block([L : [12, 24][k]],\n  printf(true, \"~3d   ~6,4f   ~12,4f   ~20,3f   ~6,3f~%\", L, pk[k][1], pk[k][1] - Tc_f,\n         L*(pk[k][1] - Tc_f)/Tc_f, pk[k][2]))$\nA_c : float((2/%pi)*(2*K_c)^2)$\nprintf(true, \"~%Peak rise from L = 12 to 24: ~,3f measured, A ln 2 = ~,3f predicted~%\",\n       pk[2][2] - pk[1][2], A_c*log(2.0))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "  L   T_peak   T_peak - T_c   L (T_peak - T_c)/T_c   c_peak\n 12   2.3262         0.0571                  0.302    1.394\n 24   2.3109         0.0417                  0.441    1.741\nPeak rise from L = 12 to 24: 0.347 measured, A ln 2 = 0.343 predicted"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Both peaks sit above $T_c$, and the larger lattice's peak sits closer. Finite-size scaling predicts that the shift halves when $L$ doubles, so that $L(T_\\text{peak} - T_c)/T_c$ stays constant. The measured shift shrinks by less than that. The peak of $c$ is flat, and a few per cent of statistical noise in each point moves the vertex by about 0.01, which is comparable to the shift itself. The rise in peak height is a steadier quantity, and it agrees with $A \\ln 2$.\n\n## Binder Cumulant [N]\n\nThe Binder cumulant $U = 1 - \\langle m^4 \\rangle / (3\\langle m^2 \\rangle^2)$ tends to $2/3$ in the ordered phase, where $m$ hardly fluctuates, and to $0$ in the disordered phase, where $m$ is Gaussian about zero. At $T_c$ it takes a universal value close to $0.611$ that does not depend on $L$ to leading order. So the curves for different sizes cross at $T_c$ without any knowledge of the exponents. The crossing comes from linear interpolation of the difference between the two curves.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "U12 : column(res12, 5)$\nU24 : column(res24, 5)$\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"darkorange\", marker_size=7, name=\"L = 12\", points(Ts, U12),\n  color=\"steelblue\", name=\"L = 24\", points(Ts, U24),\n  color=\"black\", dash=\"dash\", name=\"T_c\", lines([Tc_f, Tc_f], [0, 0.7]),\n  title=\"Binder Cumulant\", xlabel=\"T\", ylabel=\"U\", showlegend=true)$\n\nd : U24 - U12$\nfor k : 1 thru length(Ts) - 1 do\n  if Ts[k] >= 2.1 and Ts[k] < 2.5 and d[k] > 0 and d[k+1] <= 0 then block([w],\n    w : d[k]/(d[k] - d[k+1]),\n    T_x : Ts[k] + w*(Ts[k+1] - Ts[k]),\n    U_x : U12[k] + w*(U12[k+1] - U12[k]))$\nprintf(true, \"Crossing at T = ~,4f (T_c = ~,4f, difference ~,4f), U = ~,3f~%\",\n       T_x, Tc_f, T_x - Tc_f, U_x)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Crossing at T = 2.2751 (T_c = 2.2692, difference 0.0059), U = 0.609"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "application/x-maxima-plotly": [
              "{\"data\":[{\"x\":[1.5,1.8,2.0,2.1,2.2,2.25,2.3,2.35,2.4,2.45,2.5,2.6,2.8,3.1,3.5],\"y\":[0.6662932006084143,0.6649088970819415,0.6598519790437871,0.6528859602824094,0.6341311098311261,0.6185735285768676,0.5999404096201968,0.5734670490381983,0.5405055586343872,0.49830698354695635,0.4533542318583611,0.3757156996427852,0.23407200057672373,0.14290730792352402,0.08157634769744859],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"darkorange\",\"size\":7},\"name\":\"L = 12\"},{\"x\":[1.5,1.8,2.0,2.1,2.2,2.25,2.3,2.35,2.4,2.45,2.5,2.6,2.8,3.1,3.5],\"y\":[0.666571761619309,0.66616764655215,0.6649206844139359,0.6609775390730909,0.6436502252398391,0.6312605824264694,0.5873336432341372,0.5243977514688792,0.40518846346253,0.3043975457453254,0.22391744186624774,0.16340098710229545,0.07583976048363672,0.047202170187041426,0.0420253065979328],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"steelblue\",\"size\":7},\"name\":\"L = 24\"},{\"x\":[2.269185314213022,2.269185314213022],\"y\":[0.0,0.7],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"black\",\"width\":2,\"dash\":\"dash\"},\"name\":\"T_c\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Binder Cumulant\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"width\":700,\"height\":450,\"plot_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"T\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"U\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| Step | Method |\n|------|--------|\n| Metropolis rule | Symbolic: $\\Delta E = 2 s h$ takes five values, two acceptance probabilities |\n| Critical temperature | Symbolic: self-dual point $\\sinh(2K_c) = 1$ gives $T_c = 2/\\ln(1 + \\sqrt 2)$ |\n| Exact curves | Symbolic: Onsager's $m(T)$, its low-temperature series, $u(T)$ and $c = du/dT$ |\n| Simulation | Numeric: compiled Metropolis sweeps with typed arrays, $L = 12$ and $24$ |\n| Configurations | Numeric: $64 \\times 64$ snapshots below, near and above $T_c$ |\n| Verification | Energy and magnetisation match Onsager away from $T_c$ |\n| Finite-size effects | Heat-capacity peak shift and height, Binder cumulant crossing near $T_c$ |\n\nThe compiled sweep makes the Metropolis algorithm practical in Maxima. Its slow dynamics near $T_c$ still limits the lattice size; cluster algorithms such as Wolff's remove most of that slowdown.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## References\n\n- L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, *Statistical Physics, Part 1*, §151 *A two-dimensional lattice*: Onsager's solution, with the transition at $\\tanh(J/T_c) = \\sqrt 2 - 1$, equivalent to $T_c = 2J/\\ln(1+\\sqrt 2)$; the logarithmic divergence of the heat capacity, eq. (151.14); and the order parameter vanishing as $(T_c - T)^{1/8}$, eq. (151.15).\n- The full spontaneous magnetisation $(1 - \\sinh^{-4}(2J/T))^{1/8}$ is C. N. Yang's result (Phys. Rev. 85, 808, 1952).",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}